      title: "A Poem Saved from the Fire",
      body: `## One copy

Everything we know of *Beowulf*, the greatest poem in Old English, comes from a single manuscript. It was copied by two scribes around the year **1000**, in a book that also contains a life of Saint Christopher, a description of the wonders of the East, a letter of Alexander the Great to Aristotle, and a poetic version of the biblical story of **Judith**. Scholars call the book the **Nowell Codex**, after **Laurence Nowell**, a sixteenth-century antiquary who wrote his name on its first page; it is now bound with another manuscript and catalogued in the British Library as **Cotton Vitellius A.xv**.

The poem has no title in the manuscript. It was given its name by modern editors, after its hero. We do not know who composed it, where, or when. Scholars have dated its composition anywhere from the early eighth century to the early eleventh—close to the date of the manuscript itself. The question remains one of the liveliest debates in the field.

## The fire of 1731

The manuscript nearly perished. In the seventeenth century, it belonged to the great collector **Sir Robert Cotton**, whose library, with its books shelved beneath busts of Roman emperors (hence "Vitellius"), was given to the nation. On **October 23, 1731**, a fire broke out in **Ashburnham House** in Westminster, where the Cotton library was then kept. Librarians threw books out of the windows to save them. Some manuscripts were destroyed; many others were damaged. The *Beowulf* manuscript survived, but its edges were scorched, and over the following decades, as the brittle edges crumbled, letters and words were lost.

In **1787**, an Icelandic-Danish scholar, **Grímur Jónsson Thorkelin**, who believed the poem was a record of Danish history, had a copy made of the manuscript and made another himself. These **Thorkelin transcripts** preserve some two thousand letters that have since crumbled away. Thorkelin's own edition, the first, was nearly lost too: his work was destroyed when the British bombarded Copenhagen in **1807**, and he had to begin again. It was finally published in **1815**. In the nineteenth century, the manuscript's pages were mounted in paper frames to prevent further loss. Today, scholars can study it in high-resolution digital images, and with ultraviolet and other imaging techniques that reveal letters invisible to the naked eye.

## Why so little?

*Beowulf* is one of only four major manuscripts of Old English poetry. The others are the **Exeter Book**, given to Exeter Cathedral by Bishop Leofric in the eleventh century, which contains the elegies *The Wanderer* and *The Seafarer* and nearly a hundred riddles; the **Vercelli Book**, which somehow found its way to a cathedral in northern Italy and contains *The Dream of the Rood*; and the **Junius Manuscript**, with poems on biblical subjects. Altogether, some thirty thousand lines of Old English poetry survive. Much more must have been lost—in the Viking raids, the Norman Conquest, the dissolution of the monasteries in the sixteenth century, and through the simple neglect of a language that later readers could no longer understand.

## Who were the Anglo-Saxons?

The people who composed and listened to *Beowulf* were the **Anglo-Saxons**, the descendants of Germanic peoples—**Angles**, **Saxons**, and **Jutes**, according to the historian **Bede**—who crossed the North Sea from what is now northern Germany and Denmark in the fifth and sixth centuries, after the end of Roman rule in Britain, and settled in what became England (the land of the Angles). They brought their language, the ancestor of English, and their religion, the worship of gods such as **Woden** and **Thunor**, whose names survive in Wednesday and Thursday.

Beginning in **597**, when Pope Gregory the Great sent the monk **Augustine** to Kent, and from Ireland through missionaries such as **Aidan** in Northumbria, the Anglo-Saxons converted to Christianity. By the late seventh century, England was a Christian land with great monasteries, such as **Jarrow**, where Bede wrote his *Ecclesiastical History of the English People* (731), and **Lindisfarne**, where the Lindisfarne Gospels were made. The Anglo-Saxon kingdoms—Northumbria, Mercia, Wessex, East Anglia, Kent—were united into a single kingdom of England in the tenth century, after the struggle against the Vikings led by **Alfred the Great** (r. 871–899) and his successors.

## A Christian poem about pagans

*Beowulf* is set not in England but in **Scandinavia**, in the fifth and sixth centuries, among the **Danes**, the **Geats** (a people of southern Sweden), and the **Swedes**—the ancestral world of the Anglo-Saxons before their migration and conversion. Its characters are pagans. Yet the poet is a Christian, who refers to God, the Creation, Cain and Abel, and the Flood, and who presents his heroes as noble men who lived before the light of the Gospel reached them.

Some of the poem's characters are historical. The Geatish king **Hygelac**, Beowulf's uncle, is mentioned by the Frankish historian **Gregory of Tours**, writing in the late sixth century, as a king named Chlochilaicus who was killed while raiding the Frisian coast around **520**—a raid that *Beowulf* mentions several times. The Danish kings Hrothgar and Halga, and the Swedish kings Ohthere and Onela, appear in Scandinavian legends. The poem's world is a blend of history, legend, and imagination.

## How to read it

This course reads *Beowulf* in modern English translation, with attention to the original. The most widely read translation is that of the Irish poet **Seamus Heaney** (1999), which won the Whitbread Book of the Year award. Others include those of **J. R. R. Tolkien** (prose, published in 2014), **Maria Dahvana Headley** (2020), and **Roy Liuzza** (2nd ed., 2013). Each translation makes different choices: between prose and verse, between preserving the strangeness of the original and making it accessible. Reading two translations side by side shows how much is lost, and found, in translation. Wherever possible, this lesson quotes the Old English itself, with a literal translation, so that you can hear the original voice.

> **Pause and consider.** The whole of *Beowulf* depends on one manuscript that nearly burned. What does the fragility of its survival suggest about the literature of the past we do not have?`,
