      title: "The Sound of Old English",
      body: `## A foreign language

**Old English** (sometimes called Anglo-Saxon) is the earliest form of English, spoken from the fifth century to around the twelfth. To a modern reader, it looks like a foreign language. Here are the opening lines of *Beowulf*:

> *Hwæt! We Gardena in geardagum,*
> *þeodcyninga, þrym gefrunon,*
> *hu ða æþelingas ellen fremedon.*

A literal translation: "Listen! We have heard of the glory of the Spear-Danes in days of old, of the kings of the people, how the princes performed deeds of courage."

Old English used some letters that modern English has lost: **þ** (thorn) and **ð** (eth), both pronounced "th"; **æ** (ash), pronounced like the "a" in "cat"; and the letter **ƿ** (wynn), for "w," which modern editions usually print as "w." It was a highly **inflected** language, like Latin or modern German, in which the endings of nouns, adjectives, and verbs changed to show their grammatical role in the sentence, so that word order was more flexible than in modern English.

Yet many words are recognizable once you know how to listen. *Geardagum* is "yore-days," days of old. *Cyning* is "king." *Ellen* means courage. *Fremedon* is related to "frame," meaning "performed" or "carried out." About half of the most common words in modern English—*the*, *is*, *and*, *house*, *man*, *woman*, *child*, *love*, *eat*, *drink*, *sleep*—come directly from Old English.

## "Hwæt!"

The first word of the poem, ***Hwæt***, has been translated in many ways: "Lo!", "Listen!", "Hear me!", "Attend!", "What!" Heaney famously translated it as "**So.**", capturing the tone of a storyteller in an Irish kitchen beginning a tale—a choice that some scholars praised and others disputed. Tolkien used "Lo!" The word is a call for attention, the voice of a performer beginning to speak before an audience. It reminds us that Old English poetry was meant to be **heard**, recited aloud in the hall, perhaps with the accompaniment of a harp.

## Alliterative verse

Old English poetry did not use **rhyme**, which became common in English only after the Norman Conquest, under the influence of French. Instead, it used **alliteration**: the repetition of initial sounds in stressed syllables. Each line was divided into two half-lines, separated by a pause called a **caesura**. Each half-line had two stressed syllables. The first stressed syllable of the second half-line had to alliterate with one or both of the stressed syllables of the first half-line.

Consider the line: *Grendel gongan, Godes yrre bær* ("Grendel came walking; he bore God's anger"). The stressed syllables *Gren-*, *gon-*, and *God-* all alliterate on **g**. In a famous line describing the coming of the monster: *Com on wanre niht / scriðan sceadugenga* ("Came in the dark night / striding, the shadow-walker"). Any vowel could alliterate with any other vowel.

The effect is a strong, heavy, rhythmic beat, quite different from the regular meters of later English poetry. When Heaney translated *Beowulf*, he tried to preserve something of this music, writing in loosely alliterative lines with four stresses. Alliterative verse survived in English long after the Conquest, as we will see when we read *Sir Gawain and the Green Knight*, and it was revived in the twentieth century by poets such as **W. H. Auden** and Heaney himself.

## Kennings

One of the most distinctive features of Old English poetry is the **kenning**: a compound expression that describes something indirectly, by a kind of riddling metaphor. The sea is the ***hron-rad***, "**whale-road**," or the *swan-rad*, "swan-road." The body is the ***ban-hus***, "**bone-house**." A king is a ***beag-gyfa***, "**ring-giver**." The sun is the "candle of the world." A ship is a "sea-steed" or "wave-floater." Blood is "battle-sweat." A sword is "the leavings of hammers."

Kennings compress a vivid image into a single word, and invite the listener to solve a small puzzle. They also served a practical purpose: they gave the poet many different ways of naming the same thing, which helped him meet the demands of alliteration. A poet who needed a word for "king" beginning with *b* could say *beag-gyfa*; one beginning with *g* could say *gold-wine gumena*, "gold-friend of men."

## Variation

Old English poets also used a technique called **variation**: restating the same idea in different words, piling up phrases that describe the same person or thing from different angles. When the poet introduces Hrothgar's hall, he calls it in quick succession a "mead-hall," a "great hall," a "hall-building," and "Heorot." When he describes God, he calls him "the Lord," "the Ruler of glory," "the eternal Lord," "the Creator." Variation slows the narrative, inviting the listener to linger, contemplate, and see the same thing from many sides. Modern readers sometimes find it repetitive; Anglo-Saxon audiences evidently found it rich.

## The scop

In the world of the poem, poetry is performed in the hall by a professional poet called a ***scop*** (pronounced "shop"), who sings to the harp at feasts. In *Beowulf*, Hrothgar's scop sings of the Creation on the night Heorot is completed, and later, after Beowulf's victory, improvises a poem in praise of the hero, comparing him to the dragon-slayer Sigemund. Scholars once believed that *Beowulf* itself was composed orally by such a poet, drawing on traditional formulas, in the way that the Homeric epics are thought to have been composed. The American scholar **Francis Magoun** argued in 1953 that Old English poetry was "**oral-formulaic**," using the methods that Milman Parry and Albert Lord had found among the singers of the Balkans. Others have argued that *Beowulf*, with its complex structure and learned Christian allusions, was the work of a literate poet writing in a traditional oral style. The truth may lie between: a poet who knew the oral tradition intimately and used writing to shape it.

> **Pause and consider.** The kenning "whale-road" names the sea by a creature that travels it. Try inventing three kennings of your own for things in your world. What do they reveal that the plain word conceals?`,
