      title: "The Heroic Code",
      body: `## Lord and retainer

The world of *Beowulf* is governed by what scholars call the **heroic code**, a set of values shared by the warrior aristocracies of the early Germanic peoples, which was described as early as the first century AD by the Roman historian **Tacitus** in his *Germania*. At its center was the relationship between a **lord** and his **retainers**, the band of warriors called the ***comitatus***, who lived in his hall and fought for him.

The lord's duty was **generosity**: he provided his men with food, drink, shelter, and gifts of treasure—rings, arm-rings, weapons, armor, horses. That is why kings are called "ring-givers" and "gold-friends." A good king, in *Beowulf*, is one who gives generously; a bad king, like Heremod, hoards his treasure and kills his own men. The retainers' duty was **loyalty**: to fight for their lord, to defend him, to avenge him, and, if he fell, to die with him rather than flee the battlefield. Tacitus wrote that it was a lifelong disgrace for a warrior to survive his chief in battle.

## Glory

The highest value of the heroic world was **glory**, or fame, in Old English ***lof*** and ***dom***: the reputation for courage and generosity that a man earned by his deeds, and that survived him in the memory of others, in songs and stories. Since life was short and death certain, the only immortality a warrior could hope for was the immortality of fame. As Beowulf tells Hrothgar after Grendel's mother kills his friend: "Each of us must await the end of life in this world; let him who can win glory before death. That is best for a warrior, when he is no longer living." The last word of the poem, describing Beowulf, is ***lofgeornost***, "**most eager for fame**."

## The feud

The heroic world was also governed by the law of the **feud**. When a man was killed, his kinsmen had a duty to avenge him by killing the killer or one of his kin—unless the killer's kin paid **wergild** ("man-price"), a compensation in money set according to the rank of the victim. The system of wergild was intended to limit violence, by offering an alternative to revenge. But it often failed, and feuds could continue for generations.

*Beowulf* is full of feuds. The poem's many digressions—the Finnsburh episode, the stories of the wars between the Geats and the Swedes, the future feud between Hrothgar and his son-in-law Ingeld—describe feuds that destroy dynasties and peoples. Grendel himself is described as refusing to pay wergild for those he has killed. The poem presents the feud as both a necessary duty and a cycle of destruction.

## The poet's view

What does the *Beowulf* poet think of the heroic code? Scholars have disagreed. Some see the poem as a celebration of heroic values, presented as noble even if pagan. Others see it as a Christian critique of those values, showing how the pursuit of glory and the cycle of revenge lead to destruction. The poet clearly admires courage, loyalty, and generosity. But he also shows their limits: the impermanence of earthly glory, the futility of the feud, the dangers of pride and greed.

The clearest statement of the poet's view comes in a long speech by Hrothgar after Beowulf has killed Grendel's mother, which scholars call "**Hrothgar's sermon**," which we will read in the next lesson. In it, the old king warns Beowulf against pride: "Do not give way to pride, … famous warrior. The glory of your strength lasts but a little while."

## Christianity and paganism

One of the central questions about *Beowulf* is how its Christian elements relate to its pagan, heroic world. The poet refers frequently to God, the Creator, the Ruler of glory, and his characters—Hrothgar and Beowulf—speak of God's providence, thank him for their victories, and acknowledge his power. Yet they never mention Christ, the Church, the sacraments, or salvation. The Old Testament is present (Cain, the Flood, the Creation), but not the New. The characters are monotheists who know God, but not Christians.

The poet seems to present his characters as **virtuous pagans**, who lived before the coming of Christianity to the North, and who knew God through natural reason and the order of creation, but not through revelation—a common idea in the early Middle Ages. He condemns the Danes' sacrifices to idols as the error of heathens who "did not know the Lord God." He leaves open the question of whether his heroes are saved. When Beowulf dies, his soul goes "to seek the judgment of the righteous"—a phrase that has been interpreted both optimistically and as deliberately ambiguous.

## The audience

Why would a Christian poet in Anglo-Saxon England compose a long poem celebrating pagan heroes? Perhaps because the Anglo-Saxon aristocracy, though Christian, still valued the heroic ideals of their ancestors, and still traced their descent from pagan kings—the royal genealogies of Anglo-Saxon kingdoms went back to Woden. A famous letter of **Alcuin**, the English scholar at Charlemagne's court, written in **797** to an English bishop, rebuked monks for listening to heroic songs at their meals: "**What has Ingeld to do with Christ?**" he asked. "The house is narrow; it cannot hold both." Ingeld is a character mentioned in *Beowulf*. Alcuin's complaint shows that monks, as well as warriors, loved such stories. *Beowulf* may be the work of a poet who believed, unlike Alcuin, that the house was wide enough for both.

> **Pause and consider.** Alcuin asked, "What has Ingeld to do with Christ?" How might the *Beowulf* poet answer him?`,
